edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186335779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229139 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186348246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229139 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186433342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229139 #edb/ref 1039 "Using Linearity Properties of Linear Differential Operators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229139 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186228951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229139 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "68148c93-9139-5deb-8a53-a05d63ec5930" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186229998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Differential Operators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186229993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229264 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "02989bdb-eb33-5669-a5fd-ba6ae2e8e96d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229993 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229994 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229993 #edb/ref 1061 591452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229993 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "84f3814e-98a9-5834-ac22-59909528ef84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229993 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229994 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229995 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229994 #edb/ref 1061 591453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229994 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "217461ee-fef0-5c90-a0c9-17bb7b4eec7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229994 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230211 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229995 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229995 #edb/ref 1061 591454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229995 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "60f2a01e-93e7-50c5-9aeb-84890396ee17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229995 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229137 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229996 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229996 #edb/ref 1061 591457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229996 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "93ca49af-1d89-5a5d-8223-c645852994cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229996 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229997 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186229998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229997 #edb/ref 1061 591458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229997 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c4cc306-2777-59aa-b11f-63020aec529d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229997 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229138 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229998 #edb/ref 1061 591461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229998 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "11bd78b5-5bc9-5c73-a208-5e08533f0ffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229998 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229139 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230210 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that $D$ is an $n$th-order differential operator, and the functions $y, y_1,$ and $y_2$ are $n$-times differentiable in some domain of interest. \n\nWe say that a differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following conditions hold:\n\n- $D(a y(x)) = a D(y(x)),$ where $a$ is a constant.\n\n- $D(y_1(x) + y_2(x)) = D(y_1(x)) + D(y_2(x)).$\n\nWe can combine these two conditions into a single condition as follows:\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIt's worth taking a moment to compare these conditions to those we used when defining a *linear transformation*\n\n$$\n\\mathbf T: \\mathbf v \\rightarrow \\mathbf w.\n$$\n\n \n\nFor $\\mathbf T$ to be linear, it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(a\\mathbf{v})=a\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v})$ for any vector $\\mathbf{v}$ and any scalar $a.$\n\n- $\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1+\\mathbf{v}_2)=\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_1)+\\mathbf{T}(\\mathbf{v}_2)$ for any vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2.$\n\nThe conditions are essentially the same in both cases! Only instead of a transformation $\\mathbf T,$ we have a differential operator $D.$ And instead of vectors as our inputs and outputs, we have functions.\n\nNext, we'll apply this definition to a specific differential operator to see if it is linear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230210 #edb/ref 1065 14617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230210 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7af65e32-6bf5-5cbf-868a-c08401a21ff4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230210 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230211 #edb/ref 1064 "A differential operator $D$ is **linear** if the following linearity condition is satisfied.\n\n$$\nD(ay_1(x) + by_2(x)) = aD(y_1(x)) + bD(y_2(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nLet's consider some examples:\n\n- First, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_1(y) = x\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} +x^2y\n$$\n\nThis operator is indeed linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above holds. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})=x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince derivatives distribute over sums, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{1}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1})+x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(b{y}_{2})+{x}^{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =ax\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{1})+bx\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({y}_{2})+a{x}^{2}{y}_{1}+b{x}^{2}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =a{{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{1})}_{⏟}}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{1})}+b{{(x\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}{y}_{2})}_{⏟}}_{{D}_{1}({y}_{2})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, since $D_1(ay_1 + by_2) = aD_1(y_1) + bD_1(y_2),$ the operator $D_1$ is linear.\n\n- Now, let's consider the following differential operator:\n\n$$\nD_2(y) = y\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\n$$\n\nThis operator is *not* linear. To see this, we need to check that the linearity condition above does not hold. So we start by applying our operator to the function $y = a y_1(x) + b y_2(x){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})(a\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}) \\\\  & ={a}^{2}{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}+ab{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+{b}^{2}{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to $aD_2(y_1) + bD_2(y_2),$ we see that they are *not* the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a{D}_{2}({y}_{1})+b{D}_{2}({y}_{2}) & =a{y}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{1}}{\\text{d}x}+b{y}_{2}\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{2}}{\\text{d}x}\\ne {D}_{2}(a{y}_{1}+b{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the operator $D_2$ is *not* linear." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230211 #edb/ref 1065 14620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230211 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "423cbd55-072d-53de-9269-6dc635abd107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230211 #edb/ref 1067 "The Linearity Condition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230212 #edb/ref 1064 "An **$n$th-order linear differential operator**, denoted $L$, has the general form:\n\n$$\nL(y) = f_{n}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n}y}{\\textrm d x^{n}} + f_{n-1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{n-1}y}{\\textrm d x^{n-1}} + \\cdots + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nThe functions $f_i(x)$ are called the **coefficient functions**. They must depend only on the variable $x$ and be continuous on the domain of interest. The **order** of the operator is the order of the highest derivative whose coefficient is not zero.\n\nIf all the coefficient functions $f_i(x)$ are constants, we call the operator a **linear operator with constant coefficients**.\n\nLet's look at two examples:\n\n- The operator $L(y) = x^2 y'' - (\\sin x) y' + 2y$ is a 2nd-order *linear differential operator*. Its coefficient functions are $f_2(x) = x^2$, $f_1(x) = -\\sin x$, and $f_0(x) = 2$.\n\n- The operator $L(y) = 4y''' + 7y'' - y$ is a 3rd-order *linear operator with constant coefficients*. Its coefficient functions are $f_3(x) = 4$, $f_2(x) = 7$, $f_1(x) = 0$, and $f_0(x) = -1$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230212 #edb/ref 1065 14623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230212 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ea3a57ac-0a91-5f3d-85bc-303fef4eb21f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230212 #edb/ref 1067 "The General Form of a Linear Differential Operator" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263090 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+6x-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263090 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263090 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "09b0bb28-b0fb-5e8b-b3b7-46bc10b2c6b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263426 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263426 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263426 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31948791-dcc1-59bd-a512-a8b1324e7e66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263496 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}+12x-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263496 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263496 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "375c88a1-f8d6-5547-a45f-b2cacbf90b4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263956 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263956 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263956 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6e577225-120c-5926-a558-ff3fdf544279" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265102 #edb/ref 1080 "{x}^{2}-9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265102 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265102 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ef96f515-25e7-5799-a911-9ff530d6058d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186263956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186265102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186263956 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265696 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd9965ee-4811-54a2-aa77-27dcd857b9aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186265696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130081" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265817 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose that $L$ is a linear differential operator such that $L({y}_{1})=3x-5$ and $L({y}_{2})=(x-3)^{2}$ for some functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ What is $L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2})?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90434f36-e6cb-59b9-8b48-0ed0424bb2ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186265817 #edb/ref 1074 "For any linear differential operator,\n\n$$\nL(a{y}_{1}(x)+b{y}_{2}(x))=aL({y}_{1}(x))+bL({y}_{2}(x))\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants. \n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) & =2L({y}_{1})+L({y}_{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (3x-5)+(x-3)^{2} \\\\  & =6x-10+{x}^{2}-6x+9 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+(6x-6x)+(-10+9) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308839 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308839 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308839 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d87b94e-2a58-5a91-b413-e2ace8c0acef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309184 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309184 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309184 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3977be65-6745-5b00-9e8b-2471e205c872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309500 #edb/ref 1080 "I, II, and III" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309500 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309500 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "62e91591-c9db-593a-9041-dbbc0a50e178" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309741 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309741 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186309741 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7f143cd7-e11a-59b4-bd54-f611d460cc2d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310471 #edb/ref 1080 "None" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310471 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310471 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d5ed839d-2200-51a0-8970-9289668f781e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186308839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186309741 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186310471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186309500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186310825 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "0af460f7-9e3d-5968-8b8f-b6d594daad31" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311885 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186310825 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311885 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337778" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311885 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311885 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are linear differential operators?\n\nI. ${D}_{1}(y)=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\,y$\n\nII. ${D}_{2}(y)=\\sqrt{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y$\n\nIII. ${D}_{3}(y)=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{e}^{x}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}\\,y$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311885 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a6ed137-ea67-591f-bf1a-a0d1b0037ea7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186311885 #edb/ref 1074 "An $n$th-order differential operator $L$ is linear if it is of the following form:\n\n$$\nL(y)={f}_{n}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}+{f}_{n-1}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n-1}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{n-1}}+⋯+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwhere each ${f}_{i}$ is a function of $x$ only. \n\nWith that in mind, let's check each operator.\n\n- Operator ${D}_{1}$ is linear. It is a first-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{1}(y)={f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{1}(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{2}$ is linear. It is a second-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{2}(y)={f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{2}(x)=\\sqrt{x},{f}_{1}(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x),$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-2.$\n\n- Operator ${D}_{3}$ is linear. It is a third-order linear differential operator of the form\n\n$$\n{D}_{3}(y)={f}_{3}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}+{f}_{2}(x)\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{f}_{1}(x)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{f}_{0}(x)y\n$$\n\nwith ${f}_{3}(x)=2,{f}_{2}(x)={e}^{x},{f}_{1}(x)=0,$ and ${f}_{0}(x)=-\\frac{1}{1+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186433349 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1045 "Linear Differential Operators" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186433347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433323 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "083d5c44-88fc-52b7-9ce7-a90d46faa793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433324 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433324 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433324 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "16f1d4a5-53db-5cf6-bc1b-078445acab5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433325 #edb/ref 1080 "9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433325 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433325 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1affdab9-1b52-5da7-9d2f-106390720503" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433326 #edb/ref 1080 "I and II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433326 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433326 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1df3fa27-db87-5d4f-a0be-23a81125c98c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433327 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433327 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433327 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "567e86ad-0a32-55a9-8b19-931ce53e8d20" #edb/ref 175921860